For Instance

    You've learned about types in Python and how they're similar to types of things in the real world. 
    In Python, a type serves as the blueprint or definition for creating objects, which are the individual instances.

    For every type in the real world, there are many actual physical instances (objects):

        For the type Car, there are countless individual variations on the road, each with its own: 
            mileage history, make, model, color, fuel level, scratches, and dents. 

        These are the object's properties (or attributes). Each car also has a particular set of behaviors (or methods): 
            its own ways for starting, braking, shifting, and the like.

        For the type City, each instance of a city has a different name, population, location, and landmarks. 
        These are its attributes. 
        And a city has certain methods for getting things done like responding to emergencies or collecting garbage.

    The type defines or describes the properties (attributes) and behaviors (methods) of a particular kind of thing. 
    But each concrete example of the type — each car, each city — is a separate, independent instance (object) of that type.

    Imagine two cars of the same make and model, built in the same year. 
    One is speeding down the highway on a trip; the other is out of gas by the side of the road. 
    Same type (Car), but two different instances (objects).

    Or think of two cities. The location of each city will be different. 
    A landmark in one city might draw lots of tourists, but a landmark in another might be in disrepair and get few visitors. 
    Both are instances of the City type, but each individual city object can be very different from the next.

    In programming, specifically in Python, you can create and use different instances (objects) of a given type. Each instance has its own set of property values (attribute values), and each instance can perform behavior (methods) independent of other instances.

